  {
   "id": "4KEooMieXlgJ",
   "from": "Christian Bartels",
   "author": "11cd7b0c99a3bee8",
   "date": "2014-05-16T16:25:51Z",
   "subject": "difference: x  ~ normal(m, s) versus lp <- normal_log(x,m,s)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>I try to understand differences between log likelihood calculated by</div><div><br></div><div>x  ~ normal(m, s);</div><div><br></div><div>and </div><div><br></div><div>lp &lt;- normal_log(x,m,s)<br></div><div><br></div><div>My expectation was that the two should differ only by a constant. This is not what I get (below). What do I missunderstand?</div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-EihCMAfL22I/U3Y6Ju95s_I/AAAAAAAAFf0/kjbmiBA2BYw/s1600/test.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-EihCMAfL22I/U3Y6Ju95s_I/AAAAAAAAFf0/kjbmiBA2BYw/s1600/test.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792466551853000&amp;usg=AOvVaw28MZXtmG1RJHtjN1dIE2hS\"><img src=\"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-EihCMAfL22I/U3Y6Ju95s_I/AAAAAAAAFf0/kjbmiBA2BYw/s400/test.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"257\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Model is </p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">data {</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  int&lt;lower=0&gt; N;</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  vector[N] x;</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">} </font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">parameters {</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  real m;</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; s;</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">} </font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">model {</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    x  ~ normal(m, s);  // fit</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">} </font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">generated quantities { </font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    real lp;</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    lp &lt;- normal_log(x,m,s);</font></p><p style=\"clear:both\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">}</font></p><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">code and documentation is attached.</p><div>Best regards,</div><div><br></div><div>Christian</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "REJETvsYs2wJ",
   "from": "Marcus Brubaker",
   "author": "2a7f346506a3e9ff",
   "date": "2014-05-16T16:32:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] difference: x ~ normal(m, s) versus lp <- normal_log(x,m,s)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">There is an additional term that is needed because Stan (under the hood) does a non-linear reparameterization of s in order to enforce the lower bound.  To correct for that, the log determinant of the transformation Jacobian is added to lp__.  See chapter 17 of the Stan manual for more on this.<div>\r\n<br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Marcus</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, May 16, 2014 at 12:25 PM, Christian Bartels <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hanschrist...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
